    <!-- How Pingers keeps you private -->
    <string name="security_title">How Pingers keeps you private</string>
    <string name="sec_intro">Pingers is built so that the people running it can\'t see where you are, who you meet, or what you say. Here\'s how, and where the limits are.</string>
    <string name="sec_party_title">Your Party is locked to its members</string>
    <string name="sec_party_body">Party names, members and Spots are encrypted on your phone. Only members\' phones hold the keys. The safety code you compare when joining proves nobody slipped in between you.</string>
    <string name="sec_finding_title">Finding friends without telling the server</string>
    <string name="sec_finding_body">Your phone posts anonymous check-ins that only your Party can recognise, and they look different every time. Your phone, not the server, spots a friend in the same Spot, and only for the Spot you\'re in.</string>
    <string name="sec_waking_title">Waking a friend\'s phone</string>
    <string name="sec_waking_body">The alert goes through a separate push relay. The server knows an alert was sent but not to which phone. The relay knows a phone was woken but not why or by whom. Turn on \"No wake-up pushes\" and nobody sees it at all.</string>
    <string name="sec_address_title">Hiding your internet address</string>
    <string name="sec_address_body">Everything anonymous, including live locations in a meetup, travels through a privacy relay run by someone else. The relay sees your address but not what you send; the server sees what arrives but not from where.</string>
    <string name="sec_meetups_title">Meetups are just for who was there</string>
    <string name="sec_meetups_body">Each meetup gets brand-new keys shared only by the people in it. Party members who weren\'t there can\'t read it, people who join later don\'t see earlier messages, and when everyone leaves, every phone deletes the chat and keys.</string>
    <string name="sec_exact_title">Your exact spot is yours to share</string>
    <string name="sec_exact_body">In a meetup, others see you\'re there, not where. Your position is locked with your own key, which you hand only to people you approve. Others can\'t even tell whether you\'ve shared with anyone.</string>
    <string name="sec_pauses_title">Pauses that look like nothing</string>
    <string name="sec_pauses_body">Focus, Do Not Disturb and your exclusion zones are checked on your phone and never leave it. While paused, your phone keeps sending blank check-ins, so being paused looks exactly like being somewhere else.</string>
    <string name="sec_account_title">No phone number, no tracking ID</string>
    <string name="sec_account_body">Your account is a key made on your phone. To stop spam without knowing who you are, your phone proves \"I\'m a real member\" mathematically, without saying which member. Nothing is kept as a location history.</string>
    <string name="sec_limits">What Pingers can\'t protect</string>
    <!-- Bold lead | rest of the sentence -->
    <string-array name="sec_limits_items">
        <item>Party members you trust.|Any member could claim to be at a Spot, or use a modified app to count how many people are at one. Only invite people you\'d meet anyway.</item>
        <item>People you share with.|Someone you approve can screenshot where you were. Stopping sharing locks them out from then on.</item>
        <item>Operators working together.|If the server and the push relay pooled their logs, they could work out who met whom, but still not where.</item>
        <item>Apple and Google.|They see that a notification arrived, not what it says. \"No wake-up pushes\" stops this.</item>
        <item>An unlocked, stolen phone.|It reveals your Parties, Spots and exclusion zones. Ask a member to remove the lost phone.</item>
    </string-array>
    <string name="sec_hood">Under the hood</string>
    <!-- What | how -->
    <string-array name="sec_hood_items">
        <item>Party and meetup encryption|MLS · RFC 9420</item>
        <item>Privacy relay|Oblivious HTTP · RFC 9458, chunked</item>
        <item>Sealed push tokens, location keys|HPKE · RFC 9180</item>
        <item>Anonymous membership proofs|RLN zero-knowledge proofs</item>
        <item>Fallback rate limiting|Privacy Pass · RFC 9578</item>
        <item>Post-quantum option|X-Wing hybrid</item>
    </string-array>
